# Yam launches the ADE, drives it, and quits it (T11.2, LLD §13.9).
#
# The Validate item this file *is*:
#
#   > A flow that launches the ADE, opens the fixtures project through its
#   > Recent list, reads the Flows toolbar, and quits, green through the AX
#   > adapter three times running.
#
# There is no "launch" step and there should not be: launching is how a desktop
# *session opens* (`app.launch`), so this flow starts with the application in
# front of it exactly as a web flow starts with a page. Quitting **is** a step,
# because a flow that stops using an application should be able to say so, and
# because "it quit and stayed quit" is worth failing on — a signal that ends a
# main process where it stands leaves whatever it had spawned behind (P10-F1).
#
# The first five sentences are `ade-screen.flow`'s, which is the flow *both*
# adapters run. They are written twice rather than composed because a `compose:`
# would put the quit into the shared run block too, and the whole distinction
# here is which steps a web adapter can be asked to do.
#
# ## The number in the file name
#
# `yam run` executes a project's `test:` blocks in file order, and one
# session serves the whole run — so a flow that ends with `Quit the app` has to
# be the last one, or every flow after it drives an application that is not
# there. `99-` says that out loud; `01-` and `02-` say the rest may run in any
# order.

story: the ADE opens a project, shows its flows, and quits
  The first recent project should be visible
  Click the first recent project
  The Flows rail item should be visible
  Click the Flows rail item
  The toolbar title should contain "Flows"
  Quit the app

test: the ADE lifecycle
  the ADE opens a project, shows its flows, and quits
